<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 19, 2023">
<CPscript title="Manipulating Combinatorial 'Patterns'" level="min" >
<description>This script samples from the  "combinatorial" manipulations available in CirclePack, adding vertices, adjoining complexes, slitting/zipping, hex refining, etc. Circles aid in display, but the emphasis is on combinatorics --- the prescribed tangency patterns for packings. Open the script to follow the actions.</description>
<text> Our concern here is with abstract complexes --- combinatorics only. To display them, however, we use circle packings. As you go through the script you can press 'c' if you would like to display the circles behind a complex. </text>
<Section title="The 'Seed' command">
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="Start over" iconname="idea.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map -x;seed 7;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b </cmd>
<text> In "CirclePack", packings must have interior circles. Thus, the simplest packing is a "flower", a central circle with an eclosing chain of "petal" circles (at least 3). The "seed" command builds such flowers, and is one starting point when building packings from scratch (see also 'create'). This opening command has created a 7-seed to get us started. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Adding/deleting vertices">
<text> The next two commands add 2 boundary circles to vertex 8,
then 'enclose' so 8 becomes an interior. On the canvas "Active cursors" button is an "Add bdry circle" mode: clicking l-mouse on a boundary circle adds a neighbor, clicking m-mouse closes up. Try it a few times; you can use the "Repack/Layout/Display'" menu selection to improve the geometric layout.(Be sure to return to the "default" cursor mode when you're done.) </text>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">act 0;add_cir 8;add_cir 8;disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">act 0;enclose 0 8;repack;layout;disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b; </cmd>
<text> One can add a whole generation of new vertices around the boundary. The next two commands illustrate: first a generation is added in such a way that all the previous boundary vertices become degree (at least) 7, then another generation is added so the previous become degree (at least) 6. </text>
<cmd iconname="format_increaseindent.png">act 0;add_gen 1 7;repack;layout;set_screen -a;disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b </cmd>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">act 0;add_gen 1 6;repack;layout;set_screen -a;disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b; </cmd>
<text> One can also delete vertices one at a time, but only boundary vertices or those having just three neighbors. The next commands illustrate vertex deletion at a few spots around the boundary; there is also a "Delete circle" cursor so you can zap vertices with l-mouse clicks. </text>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_online.png">act 0;set_vlist b;rm_cir vlist[1];Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="network.png">act 0;rm_cir vlist[10];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_offline.png">act 0;rm_cir vlist[14];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="run.png">act 0;rm_cir 34;disp -wr; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Slits and Zips">
<text> One can 'slit' into the interior of a packing on some edgepath and open it up; this next command illustrates an edgepath and the following command slits along these edges. Note that vertices along the edge must be "cloned" because a copy is needed on each side when the slit opens. </text>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">act 0;set_screen -d;infile_read zip.p;set_vlist 63 30 9 8;Disp -w -f -tfc1 i -tfc195 b -et8c20 e vlist; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">act 0;slit vlist;adjust_rad 1.15 8;repack;layout;disp -wr;disp -et8c20 b(67 63); </cmd>
<text> Reversing this process, we can also 'zip' together boundary edges, absorbing vertices into the interior. The next command displays the edges, and the next four commands do the zipping one edge at a time.. </text>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">act 0;set_vlist b(64 36);disp -wr;disp -et6c195 e vlist; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="list.png">zip 1 11;repack;layout;disp -wr;disp -et6c190 e vlist 32 33; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">zip 1 32;repack;layout;disp -wr;disp -et6c190 e vlist 64 33; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">zip 1 64;repack;layout;disp -wr;disp -et6c190 e vlist 62 33; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">zip 1 62;repack;layout;disp -wr;disp -et6c190 e vlist; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Cookie Cutting">
<text> A handy way to get a new complex is to cut out a region of another, as shown here. We start with a complex and a superimposed path. (Paths can be read from files, as here, created by the user, or set in the "Function" window.) The next command then cuts out the portion inside the blue path. </text>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">act 0;set_screen -d;infile_read zip.p;infile_path cutpath.g;Disp -w -f -tfc1 i -tfc195 b -g </cmd>
<cmd iconname="format_increaseindent.png">act 0;cookie;disp -wr; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Reflecting, doubling, and adjoining">
<text> The next command loads a complex in p1 and a copy with flipped orientation in p2. Corresponding edges are indicated in red. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">act 1;Cleanse;Map 1 2;infile_read blob.p;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b -et8c190 b(38 5);copy 2;act 2;reorient;layout;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b -et8c190 b(5 38); </cmd>
<text> We carry out an 'adjoin' command which combines the two complexes by identifying corresponding vertices along the marked boundary segments. The result is put in p1 (and repacked for display purposes). </text>
<cmd iconname="kghostview.png">act 1;set_Elist b(38 5);adjoin 1 2 5 5 8;repack;layout;set_screen -a;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b -et8c190 Elist; </cmd>
<text> Pack 2 was used in the previous 'adjoin', but remains unchanged itself. We can accomplish the same result as before with a 'double' of p2 across that same boundary segment, as in the next command. </text>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">act 2;set_Elist b(5 38);double b(5 38);set_aim -d;repack;layout;set_screen -a;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b -et8c190 Elist; </cmd>
<text> Now that we have these two, we can illustrate a more general "weld" process. We start by marking a boundary edge segment in blue in each of p0 and p1 --- each segment has 6 vertices. The following command then welds these two complexes together, identifying the blue edges in one with the blue edges in the other. </text>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">act 1;set_elist b(25 68);disp -et8c2 elist;disp -p2 -wr;disp -p2 -ffc90 a -et8c2 b(63 55); </cmd>
<cmd iconname="centrejust.png">act 1;adjoin 1 2 68 63 5;repack;alpha 112;layout;scale 1.5;set_screen -a;Disp -w -f -ffc90 Iv a(70 300) -tfc5 i -tfc195 b -et8c2 elist -et8c190 38 39 39 20 20 13 13 6 6 14 14 30 30 23 23 5 74 92 92 99 99 83 83 75 75 82 82 89 89 108 108 107; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Hex refinements">
<text> We find later in the chapter that we want to "refine" circle packings --- that is, we want packings with more and smaller circles, for example, to better fill a region as in "DiscreteRMT.xmd". The notion of "hexagonal" refinement of complexes is very handy. Once starts with a given complex and adds a new vertex to each edge; in each face, add three new edges connecting its new vertices. This breaks every original face into 4 in the new complex. It's called "hex" refinement because all the new vertices end up with degree 6. This can be done with any complex, but to follow the action, we'll start with the dodecahedral triangulation of the sphere, then apply two 'hex_refine" operations. Press 'h' for repeated refinement, but be careful --- the complexity goes up rapidly and will slow 'CirclePack' down. As before, press 'd' to see the circles. </text>
<cmd iconname="dnd_multi.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read dodecSph.p;Disp -w -f -tft2c5 a;copy 1;Disp -p1 -w -f -tft2c5 a;set_Vlist a; </cmd>
<cmd name="h" mnemonic="h" tooltip="Tool: 'hex_refine'" iconname="mozilla.png">act 0;hex_refine;max_pack;Disp -w -f -tft2c2 Vlist; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">[h] </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Edge flips">
<text> Among the various miscellaneous commands for changing combinatorics of a complex, one of the most interesting is the edge "flip". We start with side-by-side small hex packings to illustrate. The next command does a flip, highlighting the edges and faces involved. Comparing, you should see that in p1 an edge has been 'flipped': that is, it has been removed and replaced by a new edge in the union of its two faces. Note that the circles underlying the complex will be adjusted, so the complex will change shape. (As usual, 'd' will show you the circles.) </text>
<cmd iconname="xeyes.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read shex.p;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b;copy 1;act 1;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b;act 0;disp -p0 -ffc90 4 5 -et8c2 1 6;disp -p1 -ffc90 4 5 -et8c2 1 6; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">act 1;flip 1 6;repack;layout;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b -ffc90 4 19 -et8c190 5 7; </cmd>
<text> You can now continue such flipping in p0 or p1 (though the two will no longer be in sync). Simply place the cursor near an interior edge and press 'f'. Try it out! </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" tooltip="display the underlying circles" iconname="kivio.png">disp -c </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="f" mnemonic="f" tooltip="flip a chosen edge" iconname="kuickshow.png">set_elist z #XY;flip elist;repack;layout;Disp -w -f -tfc5 i -tfc195 b; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
    <AboutImage name="AboutImage.jpg">

    </AboutImage>
